Two rounds. Round 1 ran on deb8717..d8a2993 and its findings are recorded in
de329ee's own message (the HIGH: a level-end drop that undid itself in the
same call, found independently by both reviewers). This note is round 2: two
independent read-only reviewers on main...de329ee in parallel -- A on
correctness and the engine addresses, which it re-derived from
pristine/TotalA.exe.pristine rather than taking from the diff; B on the
synchronisation alone, asked for the interleaving that breaks each claimed
ordering rather than an opinion on the design. Acted on in 1c542a2.

BOTH FOUND THE SAME MEDIUM, INDEPENDENTLY, and it was the previous round's
defect in a narrower window. The level stamp was read AFTER the copy, and the
comment above it asserted that side was the safe one. It is the opposite:
labelling a capture that straddled a teardown with the NEW level is exactly
what makes both readers accept it -- the adopt matches because s_dropSeen has
just advanced to that value, the serve matches because s_dropReq is that
value, and the dying level's last frame then stands as the new level's
reference for the whole loading screen, where no in-play draw runs to displace
it. Fixed by reading the counter before the copy and discarding a capture the
level moved under.

A's SECOND MEDIUM is the one worth keeping: the new site's "invariant,
MEASURED" was 24 one-shot double-reads, and at the OLD site's measured 1.35 %
tear rate P(0 torn in 24) = 0.72 -- so the figure was fully consistent with a
site tearing exactly as badly as the one it was tabled against. That is this
landing's own recorded lesson about a 20-sample probe, applied to its
replacement. Fixed by giving the oracle a continuous mode
(tagpu_surfcheck.on) and re-measuring on the binary that lands:
0 of 21 669 checks torn, 18 268 in one level with seven units patrolling plus
3 401 after a full teardown and a fresh level, against the old site's 223 of
16 500.

